{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 30, :comments ["<b>Lest you be ensnared.</b> Onkelos rendered it as a term of מוֹקֵשׁ. But I say he did not attend to precision in the language, for we do not find a nun in the stem יוֹקֵשׁ — even in the root from which it would derive. But in the stem of טֵרוּף and קִשְׁקוּשׁ we do find a nun: ״וְאַרְכֻּבָתֵהּ דָא לְדָא נָקְשָׁן״ (Daniel 5). And this too I say: ״פֶּן תִּנָּקֵשׁ אַחֲרֵיהֶם״ — lest you be swept after them, to be entangled after their deeds. And likewise (Psalms 109) ״יְנַקֵּשׁ נוֹשֶׁה לְכָל אֲשֶׁר לוֹ״ — he curses the wicked man that many creditors come upon him, and they pursue and close in after his property." "<b>After their destruction from before you.</b> After you see that I destroy them from before you — you should give thought: why were these destroyed? Because of the corrupt deeds in their hands. So you too shall not do the like, lest others come and destroy you (see Sifrei)." "<b>How they served.</b> Since punishment for idolatry applies only to slaughter, incense, libation, and prostration, as it is written (Exodus 22) ״בִּלְתִּי לַה׳ לְבַדּוֹ״ — things done for the Most High — Scripture comes and teaches you here that if the manner of an idol's worship is by something else, such as פּוֹעֵר לִפְעוֹר and throwing a stone to Merkulis, that is its worship and he is liable. But slaughter, incense, libation, and prostration — even not in its customary manner, he is liable (Sanhedrin 60a)."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:46:56.677101428Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}